jmp-ai-music-player (1.2.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Make the GNOME global media keys actually reach the player.  A binding
    used to call "music-cli", but a desktop shortcut starts outside the shell
    that started the app: a source install, a .deb and a player inside a
    container can all expose a different "music-cli", so the key drove the
    wrong socket (or none) while the shortcut still looked installed.
  * Bindings now call "jmp-media-action".  It tries the running server's
    loopback API first (the URL the window or the MPRIS agent recorded), then
    the MPRIS player on the session bus, and only then music-cli.  --probe
    reports which route would work right now.
  * Settings -> Playback -> Global media keys gained a Test button that shows
    the live route, and it names a key that another GNOME shortcut already
    owns instead of claiming the binding works.
  * An entry left by 1.2.0/1.2.1 (right F5-F8, but calling music-cli) is shown
    as "outdated" and rewritten in place when the window opens or Install is
    clicked.  Nothing new is installed silently: only JMP's own bindings are
    touched, and only to point them at the helper.
  * Installing no longer reports success when the desktop refused the grab.
    gsd-media-keys and mutter can fail a grab silently; the install reads the
    user journal and says so with the exact reason.
  * The shortcut log line carries the route but never the API token, and the
    log file is created private (0600).
  * No music file, tag or playlist is touched; a key press still runs exactly
    one transport command.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 23:30:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.2.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the GNOME global media-key bindings.  They were written as "<F5>"
    instead of the GTK key name "F5".  In an accelerator string the angle
    brackets introduce a modifier, so GNOME ignored the shortcut and the keys
    still worked only while the JMP window had focus.
  * The bindings now use bare F5-F8.  An existing installation is repaired by
    the next "Install global shortcuts" click (or jmp-media-keys --install):
    old entries are recognised by name and rewritten in place, not duplicated.
  * Old entries are reported as "window only" until repaired, so the Install
    button appears again instead of claiming a broken binding works.
  * No music file, tag or playlist is touched; a key press still runs exactly
    one transport command.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The global media keys are now a setting instead of a terminal job.
    Settings -> Playback gained a "Global media keys" section that reads the
    live GNOME state, installs the four bindings in one click, removes them
    again, and keeps every other custom shortcut untouched.  The music server
    runs in a container without a session bus, so the native window performs
    the gsettings calls and answers the page through the jmphost bridge.
  * One notice at start says the keys are focus-only, and the first real key
    press offers "Set up global keys" once, with the tradeoff in plain words.
    Nothing is installed silently and pressing a key again does not nag.
  * No music file, tag or playlist is touched; a key press still runs exactly
    one transport command.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 21:10:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.1.10-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The media row of this keyboard sends plain F5-F8, which Wayland delivers
    only to the focused window, so the keys worked only while JMP had focus.
    A new helper, jmp-media-keys, registers the same four keys as GNOME global
    shortcuts that call music-cli, which makes them work in every application
    (F5 stop, F6 previous, F7 play/pause, F8 next).
  * The bindings are exclusive on purpose: while they exist, F5-F8 no longer
    reach other applications.  jmp-media-keys --remove restores the previous
    state exactly, and --status shows what is registered.
  * No music file, tag or playlist is touched: a key press runs one transport
    command.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:40:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.1.9-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 1.1.8 stopped "Continue" from jumping past the end of a shorter queue;
    this release also makes its answer tell the truth.  The reply used the
    remembered entry index, so a replayed 6-entry queue could still be
    announced as "entry 10 of 10".  It now counts the queue that really
    plays, and drops the entry line when the index no longer fits.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:05:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.1.8-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix "Continue" landing on silence when the remembered queue was longer
    than the one mpv really held: the saved entry index was applied without
    checking that the entry exists, so mpv jumped past the end and stopped.
  * The jump is now sent only when the queue really has that entry, so a
    pruned ad-hoc list or an edited playlist still continues at its first
    track instead of playing nothing.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 18:50:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.1.7-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The media row of this keyboard is stop, rewind, play/pause, forward on
    F5-F8 -- not the toggle/stop/prev/next order assumed so far.  That is why
    play/pause restarted the track (it was mapped to "previous", which seeks to
    0:00) and rewind stopped the music (it was mapped to "stop").
  * The mapping therefore follows the printed row; the same wrong guess is now
    correctable without a rebuild through
    ~/.config/julians-music-app/media-keys.json or JMP_MEDIA_KEYS.
  * The key log is timestamped and also records the action a press became and
    the player's own answer, so a key that does not do what it should is
    visible in ~/.cache/julians-music-app/media-keys.log without repeating it
    from memory.
  * Plain F5-F8 reach only the focused window (Wayland has no global key
    observation); for the keys to work in every application the keyboard row
    has to send real XF86Audio* keys, which the desktop already routes to the
    player through MPRIS.  The README explains both routes.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 18:25:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.1.6-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A media key is never a silent no-op any more.  Play/pause, back and
    forward now start what was playing last when the player is stopped
    (the same continue-last-session path as the window's Continue row), so
    the play key makes music again instead of answering "Toggled." into a
    quiet room.
  * The back key restarts the current track when it is more than three
    seconds in, or when the queue is at its first entry, instead of stopping
    at the first entry.  Forward at the end of the queue says so.
  * Pause with nothing to pause answers honestly instead of faking a pause.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:55:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.1.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The MPRIS handover between window and agent is now real: the native window
    REPLACEs the agent while it is open (it is the player that matters then) and
    the agent, which only allows replacement, asks for the name again once the
    window closes.  Neither side can steal it back from the other.
  * The agent exports its MPRIS object once instead of on every retry, so a
    long-running session cannot accumulate duplicate exports.
  * The launcher starts the agent only with an interpreter that really has
    PyGObject.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:35:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.1.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Media keys now really work on a keyboard whose media row is in function
    mode: an unmodified F5/F6/F7/F8 (and F13-F16) means play/pause, stop,
    previous, next.  Ctrl+F5 and friends stay ordinary shortcuts.
  * The MPRIS player outlives the window.  A new host agent
    (chat/media_keys.py) owns org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.jmp whenever the native
    window is closed, so the desktop's media keys still reach the player while
    the music keeps running in the container.  The window takes the name while
    it is open; the agent takes it back when the window closes and stops itself
    when the server goes away.
  * Media-key diagnosis: the window logs every key that could come from a media
    row to ~/.cache/julians-music-app/media-keys.log.  The log can only hold
    media and function keys, never typing; JMP_KEYLOG=<file> logs every key for
    a deliberate one-off.
  * Nothing new touches a file: every key is the same POST /api/transport call
    as the matching button, so no tag, playlist or audio byte changes.

 -- Julian M. Kunkel <julian.kunkel@gwdg.de>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:10:00 +0100

jmp-ai-music-player (1.1.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Media keys control the player: play/pause, next, previous and stop.
    The native window publishes an MPRIS player, so the desktop's own keys
    reach it even in the background; the focused window also handles the
    keysyms itself, and a browser window handles them while focused.
  * No tag, playlist or audio file is touched: every key becomes the same
    POST /api/transport call as the matching transport button.

